A||N||3||K2m4||86 2m4 will not start ||||1||
Z||000000||K2m4||12-09-2001||02:42 PM||||ok here is the scenerio...im driving home last night cruising at 65. The car just dies. After coming to a stop i try to start it with no avail. I can hear the fuel pump and i can smell fuel. I get the car towed home. this morning i go out change the cap rotor, tested the ignition coil tested ok. replaced the ignition module. tested the pick up coil ...it tested ok. Checked for trouble codes ...none just flashed 12 over and over.  It still ownt start. Like i said i have fuel pressure and fuel cause i can hear the pump growl and i can see the fuel wetness in the TB. can anyone offer ANY suggestions on what else i can check. I am stumped. these ignition systems arent that complictaed and rather easy to work on...so what am i missing<P>Kerm <BR>86 GT<BR>86 2M4||||reg||1
Z||000001||johnt671||12-09-2001||05:26 PM||||I had the same problem with my 84.  tried everything then gave up and put it in the shop. the problem turned out to be in the tach wiring.  After trying everything they could think of(new dist, ECM trouble shoot elec. system) they pulled the tach wire off the coil and the car started right up.  They offered to find the problem but the bill was to high.  I put in an aftermarket tach and lived happily ever after.  Maybe the tach filter was was bad.||||reg||1
Z||000002||GTDude||12-10-2001||03:59 PM||||You didn't say.......did you check for spark at the plugs.  I assume you did.  If so, unplug the pickup coil from the module and use a digital volt meter set to the a/c scale.  Probe the two unplugged wires with the meter and have someone spin the engine over.  You should get a low volt a/c reading.  If not...your pick up coil is bad.<P>------------------<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||K2m4||12-11-2001||09:15 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by GTDude:<BR><B>You didn't say.......did you check for spark at the plugs.  I assume you did.  If so, unplug the pickup coil from the module and use a digital volt meter set to the a/c scale.  Probe the two unplugged wires with the meter and have someone spin the engine over.  You should get a low volt a/c reading.  If not...your pick up coil is bad.<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Thanks gtdude...you were right my pick up coil was bad. I used your method of testing it and it showed no AC. When i used the Haynes manual way of testing it it tested good showing about .7 ohms. but heres the kicker of it all .I go tp the auto parts store and buy a new pick up coil. I go to remove the old one on the distributer, remove the pin and tried to slide out the shaft and the damn thing on the top of the distibuter that sits atop of the pick up coil is rusted on ..i couldnt get it off. After an hour of fighting i gave up and went back and purchased a whole new distributer. So a 5 dollar part turned into a 115 dollar one! sucked but the car runs like a champ now after timing the pig last night. Noticable power increase. The car definitely feels snappyier with a whole new ingnition in the car. <P>------------------<BR>Kerm<BR>86 GT<BR>86 2M4||||reg||1
